AREAS = {
 'de-pijp': ('De Pijp','Built quickly for workers in the late 19th century, De Pijp keeps its tightly packed streets beneath the cafés and small shops. Start with Albert Cuyp Market, then take the quieter streets toward Sarphatipark for coffee and record digging.','de-pijp'),
 'oud-west': ('Oud-West','Oud-West’s food scene sits beside a history of squatting and protest; some former squats remain cultural spaces. Walk Ten Katemarkt and Kinkerstraat, then look into the converted De Hallen complex rather than treating Foodhallen as the whole district.','oud-west'),
 'jordaan': ('Jordaan','Once a working-class district, the Jordaan now mixes galleries and boutiques with a lived-in neighbourhood feel. Follow its smaller streets toward Westerstraat for second-hand records, or visit Noordermarkt and the canals around Brouwersgracht.','jordaan'),
 'nine-streets': ('Nine Streets','Laid out in the 17th-century canal district, these nine narrow streets cross the grand canals between Leidsestraat and the Jordaan. Today they are compact and shop-heavy: browse small boutiques and Waxwell’s records, then step onto a bridge for the wider view.','nine-streets'),
 'centrum': ('Old Centre & Spuistraat','Amsterdam grew around the dam on the Amstel; this is the city’s oldest and busiest ground. See Dam Square as that historical hinge, then leave the main shopping stream for Spuistraat’s records and independent design.','centrum'),
 'haarlemmerbuurt': ('Haarlemmerbuurt','Haarlemmerstraat and Haarlemmerdijk trace the old westward route toward Haarlem, extended as the canal ring grew in the 1600s. The independent shopfronts and cafés make this a better small-street wander than the centre’s busiest retail strips: pair TOKI or Good Beans with a bite on Haarlemmerstraat.','haarlemmerbuurt'),
 'canal-belt': ('Canal Belt','The canal ring was a planned 17th-century expansion, not an accidental maze of picturesque waterways. Its grand houses now hold photography at Huis Marseille and Foam; Utrechtsestraat adds design shops and Concerto’s five adjoining record-store premises.','canal-belt'),
 'oost': ('Oost','Oost grew from farmland and estates into housing and industry, with later arrivals shaping its shops and food. Browse busy Dappermarkt and Javastraat, then head farther out toward Park Frankendael and De Kas for a greener stop.','oost'),
 'noord': ('Noord / NDSM','NDSM’s shipyard closed in 1984; artists and students later reclaimed its vast industrial halls. Ferry over for the cranes, waterfront and large-scale work at STRAAT, but treat Garage Noord as a separate stop, not something beside NDSM.','noord'),
 'museumkwartier': ('Museumkwartier','Rijksmuseum and Concertgebouw helped anchor this 19th-century cultural district south of the canals. Choose a museum rather than attempting them all: the Stedelijk pairs especially well with nearby contemporary-fashion browsing.','museumkwartier'),
 'de-baarsjes': ('De Baarsjes','Former western countryside became a 20th-century residential district with striking Amsterdam School brickwork. Look at Mercatorplein’s architecture before coffee on Jan Evertsenstraat; a Foam-linked photography project offers another reason to travel beyond the centre.','de-baarsjes'),
}
